WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.937
Module 2 was mostly about structure: tables, keys, relationships, and constraints.

00:00:05.937 --> 00:00:10.627
Module 3 is where we begin interacting with the data inside that structure.

00:00:10.627 --> 00:00:14.517
When we only read data, a mistake may return the wrong rows.

00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:18.892
When we start changing data, a mistake can also modify or remove rows.

00:00:18.892 --> 00:00:22.057
That is why we will use a repeatable safety process.

00:00:22.057 --> 00:00:24.167
First, define what you need.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.407
Then preview the rows that match.

00:00:26.407 --> 00:00:28.937
Use a stable identifier when possible.

00:00:28.937 --> 00:00:33.467
Execute the smallest correct statement, and inspect what PostgreSQL returns.

00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:37.097
For write operations, verification is especially important.

00:00:37.097 --> 00:00:43.802
If you are not sure what an UPDATE or DELETE will touch, run a SELECT with the same condition first.

00:00:43.802 --> 00:00:49.412
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:49.412 --> 00:00:51.217
an application safely inserting or updating business records.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:01:00.107
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:01:00.107 --> 00:01:01.602
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:01.602 --> 00:01:07.237
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:01:07.237 --> 00:01:12.257
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:12.257 --> 00:01:15.100
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
